(* LabeledSavina — the message-heavy Savina benchmarks reimplemented as a
 * parameterized IFC-monitoring study, published as a SimpleModule blob for
 * consumers to `load "LabeledSavina"` (runall.trp). Re-run this producer after
 * changing a benchmark or rebuilding the compiler:
 *
 *   mkdir -p out && ./local.sh examples/benchmarks/labeled-savina/LabeledSavina.mod.trp
 *   (or `make benchmark-modules` from the repository root).
 *
 * Unlike the other suites' descriptor sets, this one stays a blob rather than
 * a program-relative module: its harness closes over the root `authority`
 * (for `declassify` and `blockdown`), which is bound only in main-mode
 * compilation. A module compiled in library mode has no `authority`, so the
 * descriptors are captured here — where `authority` is in scope — and shipped
 * as a saved value. See the harness comments on the pc/blocking-label baseline.
 *
 * ============================ What this measures ============================
 *
 * The overhead of the information-flow monitor is not a single number: it is a
 * curve in how much *label activity* a workload generates. This module turns
 * six Savina message-passing benchmarks (pingpong, counting, threadring,
 * prodcons, chameneos, banking) into that curve by threading a labeled payload
 * through each protocol under a three-knob label configuration, and measuring
 * the same computation across the knob settings. Every run is also a *semantics
 * test* of the monitor: labels never change values, so each labeled run must
 * produce exactly the k=0 (unlabeled) result — `check` enforces this.
 *
 * ------------------------------ The three knobs ----------------------------
 *
 * Knob 1 — label diversity k in {0, 1, 2, 4, 8}.
 *   k = 0  : the honest unlabeled baseline. No `raisedTo`, no `blockdownto`, no
 *            `declassify` anywhere on the path — the *same driver code* with all
 *            labeling disabled, so the baseline pays nothing for IFC.
 *   k >= 1 : each message's payload is labeled by one of k distinct
 *            confidentiality principals, assigned round-robin by message index.
 *            Aggregating across messages therefore joins the principals'
 *            labels. With k = 1 every payload shares the one principal, so each
 *            join is p ⊔ p = p — the lattice short-circuits and monitoring is
 *            nearly free; k = 1 is the *degenerate* case. Real monitoring cost
 *            appears at k >= 2, where the joins combine *incomparable* labels
 *            (p_i ⋢ p_j and p_j ⋢ p_i) and the lattice must do genuine work; k
 *            = 4 and k = 8 widen that work.
 *
 * Knob 2 — branch-on-secret (on/off).
 *   on  : the receiver's accumulation branches on the labeled payload content
 *         (`if v > 0 then a + v else a`), so the blocking label is raised by
 *         the branch and every value computed afterwards carries the payload's
 *         label by implicit flow. This exercises the monitor's control-flow
 *         tracking.
 *   off : payloads are carried and summed by a pure label-join
 *         (`a + v`), never branched on — value labeling with no blocking-label
 *         raise.
 *
 * Knob 3 — declassification (on/off).
 *   on  : the final aggregate is declassified to public with the root authority
 *         at the collection point, inside the timed region, before it is
 *         returned — exercising the downgrade machinery on the monitored path.
 *   off : the aggregate keeps its joined confidentiality label; only the
 *         untimed one-bit verdict inside `check` is declassified so the harness
 *         can read an ok/not-ok result.
 *
 * ============================ Monitor-legality =============================
 *
 * Every variant runs to completion with ok = true under the IFC monitor. Two
 * adjustments were required and are worth stating precisely, because they are
 * the crux of making branch-on-secret and declassify legal at once:
 *
 * 1. Principals are HIGH-INTEGRITY confidentiality labels `<p_i;#root-integrity>`,
 *    not flat tagset literals `{p_i}`. A tagset `{p_i}` carries *low* integrity,
 *    so the joined data is simultaneously high-confidentiality and
 *    low-integrity: under non-malleable IFC it can be neither declassified
 *    (robustness needs high integrity) nor endorsed (transparency needs low
 *    confidentiality). It is therefore permanently non-downgradable — the
 *    declassify knob would be impossible, and even the branch-on-secret cleanup
 *    below could not run. The `<p_i;#root-integrity>` form keeps p_i as the
 *    confidentiality principal (round-robin joins are still incomparable — the
 *    lattice work is unchanged) while being downgradable with authority.
 *
 * 2. Branch-on-secret raises an actor's BLOCKING label (not its pc), and a
 *    message inherits the sender's blocking label; a raised one exceeds a plain
 *    point `receive`r's clearance, so the send would leak presence and the
 *    receive stalls. Rather than raise every actor's mailbox clearance (which
 *    leaves each terminating actor with an un-restored clearance and a
 *    non-fatal warning, and would make the driver's own self-drain
 *    undeliverable), each branching actor settles its blocking label back to
 *    the pc with `blockdown authority` immediately before it sends onward
 *    (helper `tell`). Value labels are preserved by the settle, so the
 *    accumulator keeps its joined label; only the presence channel is cleared.
 *    `blockdown` targets the *current pc* rather than a fixed `{}`, so it is
 *    correct at any baseline — in particular inside the loaded blob, whose code
 *    runs above the top-level baseline. The driver (`main`) thus receives every
 *    result through ordinary point receives, keeping the between-benchmark
 *    drain and the file writes legal. `check` likewise settles the blocking
 *    label after forming the labeled verdict.
 *
 * None of the six protocols was structurally distorted: message topologies
 * match the Savina originals. The results were re-pointed to a
 * label-transparent aggregate where the original result did not carry the
 * payload (noted per descriptor): threadring reports the accumulated hop count
 * (the token now carries a labeled accumulator) rather than the holder index,
 * and pingpong/counting/chameneos aggregate a labeled unit contribution per
 * message. prodcons keeps the item-value sum and banking keeps money
 * conservation.
 *
 * --------------------------------- Matrix ----------------------------------
 *
 * Per benchmark: the k-sweep at branch=on, declass=off (5 descriptors,
 * `<base>-k<K>`); one branch=off contrast at k=2 (`<base>-k2-nobranch`); one
 * declass=on variant at k=2 (`<base>-k2-declass`). 7 per benchmark * 6
 * benchmarks = 42 descriptors. Each descriptor carries one size (the smallest
 * Savina default) — the interesting axis is k, not n.
 *)

import List
import SimpleModule

datatype Atoms = PING | PONG | STOP | INC | GET | RESULT | TOKEN | WIRE | DONE
              | ITEM | CREDIT | DATA | SUM | SHUTDOWN | MEET | CHANGE | EXIT
              | TRANSFER | CREDITMSG | CONFIRM | GETBALANCE | BALANCE

let
    (*======================= shared labeling harness =======================*)

    (* Eight distinct high-integrity confidentiality principals; a labeled
     * value uses `<p_i;#root-integrity>` so joins are incomparable yet the
     * result is downgradable with authority (see the header). *)
    val principals = [ `<p1;#root-integrity>`, `<p2;#root-integrity>`
                     , `<p3;#root-integrity>`, `<p4;#root-integrity>`
                     , `<p5;#root-integrity>`, `<p6;#root-integrity>`
                     , `<p7;#root-integrity>`, `<p8;#root-integrity>` ]

    (* Declassification target: bottom confidentiality, kept at root integrity
     * (the data is high-integrity, so declassify only lowers confidentiality). *)
    val pub = `<; #root-integrity>`

    (* The principal for message index `idx` under diversity `k`, round-robin. *)
    fun labelAt (cfg, idx) = List.nth principals (idx mod cfg.k)

    (* Label value `v` for message `idx`. At k = 0 the value is left untouched —
     * the honest baseline emits no `raisedTo`. *)
    fun tag (cfg, idx, v) =
        if cfg.k = 0 then v else v raisedTo (labelAt (cfg, idx))

    (* Fold payload `v` into accumulator `a`. With branch=on the fold branches on
     * the labeled content (raising the pc); with branch=off it is a pure join.
     * For every payload v >= 0 both arms compute a + v, so the value is
     * label-transparent — only the label/pc behavior differs. *)
    fun combine (cfg, a, v) =
        if cfg.branch then (if v > 0 then a + v else a) else a + v

    (* Like combine, but for a signed delta that must always be applied (bank
     * balances move both ways). branch=on branches on the labeled amount's sign
     * purely to raise the pc; both arms apply the full delta, so the balance is
     * label-transparent. *)
    fun applyAmount (cfg, balance, delta) =
        if cfg.branch
        then (if delta > 0 then balance + delta else balance + delta)
        else balance + delta

    (* Restore the blocking label to the pc before an onward send, so a
     * branch-raised actor delivers to a plain point receive (a message inherits
     * the sender's blocking label; a raised one exceeds a plain receiver's
     * clearance). Branching raises the blocking label, not the pc, so
     * `blockdown authority` settles it back to the pc baseline — whatever that
     * baseline is (`{}` at top level, the module's own baseline inside the
     * loaded blob). Value labels are unaffected. A no-op unless the actor
     * actually branches. *)
    fun settle cfg =
        if cfg.branch andalso cfg.k > 0 then blockdown authority else ()

    (* Settle-then-send: the only send form used by actors that branch. *)
    fun tell cfg (dst, msg) = (settle cfg; send (dst, msg))

    (* The collection point. With declass=on the aggregate is declassified to
     * public inside the timed region; otherwise it keeps its joined label. *)
    fun collect (cfg, a) =
        if cfg.declass andalso cfg.k > 0
        then declassify (a, authority, pub) else a

    (* Turn a (possibly labeled) verdict bool into a public bool for the harness,
     * restoring main's pc afterwards. At k = 0 the verdict is already public. *)
    fun verdict (cfg, b) =
        if cfg.k = 0 then b
        else let val v = declassify (b, authority, pub)
                 val _ = blockdown authority
             in v end

    (*============================== pingpong ===============================*)
    (* Ponger echoes a labeled unit contribution derived (branch=on: via a
     * content branch) from each ping's payload; main sums the replies. Result:
     * n, matching the Savina original's pong count. *)
    fun ppMake cfg =
        let fun ponger () =
                receive [ hn (PING, v, p) =>
                            (tell cfg (p, (PONG, combine (cfg, 0, v))); ponger ())
                        , hn STOP => () ]
            fun bench n =
                let val pong = spawn ponger
                    fun loop (0, acc) = (send (pong, STOP); collect (cfg, acc))
                      | loop (k, acc) =
                          (send (pong, (PING, tag (cfg, k, 1), self ()));
                           receive [ hn (PONG, r) => loop (k - 1, acc + r) ])
                in loop (n, 0) end
        in { bench = bench, check = fn n => fn r => verdict (cfg, r = n) } end

    (*============================== counting ===============================*)
    (* The counter folds a labeled unit per INC; GET returns the count. Result:
     * n, matching the Savina original. *)
    fun ctMake cfg =
        let fun counter c =
                receive [ hn (INC, v)  => counter (combine (cfg, c, v))
                        , hn (GET, p)  => tell cfg (p, (RESULT, c)) ]
            fun bench n =
                let val cnt = spawn (fn () => counter 0)
                    fun produce k =
                        if k = 0 then ()
                        else (send (cnt, (INC, tag (cfg, k, 1))); produce (k - 1))
                    val _ = produce n
                    val _ = send (cnt, (GET, self ()))
                in receive [ hn (RESULT, c) => collect (cfg, c) ] end
        in { bench = bench, check = fn n => fn r => verdict (cfg, r = n) } end

    (*============================= threadring ==============================*)
    (* The token carries a labeled accumulator alongside its hop countdown; each
     * actor folds a labeled unit (branch=on: on the payload) and forwards. After
     * n hops the holder reports the accumulator. Result: n (re-pointed from the
     * Savina original's holder index, so the result carries the payload). *)
    fun trMake cfg =
        let val ringSize = 100
            fun ringActor main =
                receive [ hn (WIRE, next, idx) =>
                    let fun loop () =
                            receive
                              [ hn (TOKEN, 0, acc) =>
                                  (tell cfg (main, (DONE, acc));
                                   send (next, (STOP, ringSize - 1)))
                              , hn (TOKEN, k, acc) =>
                                  (tell cfg (next,
                                     (TOKEN, k - 1, combine (cfg, acc, tag (cfg, k, 1))));
                                   loop ())
                              , hn (STOP, k) =>
                                  if k > 1 then send (next, (STOP, k - 1)) else () ]
                    in loop () end ]
            fun bench n =
                let val main = self ()
                    val pids = List.map (fn _ => spawn (fn () => ringActor main))
                                        (List.range ringSize)
                    fun wire (i, rest) =
                        case rest of
                          []      => ()
                        | p :: ps =>
                            let val next = if i = ringSize - 1
                                           then List.nth pids 0
                                           else List.nth pids (i + 1)
                                val _ = send (p, (WIRE, next, i))
                            in wire (i + 1, ps) end
                    val _ = wire (0, pids)
                    val _ = send (List.nth pids 0, (TOKEN, n, 0))
                in receive [ hn (DONE, acc) => collect (cfg, acc) ] end
        in { bench = bench, check = fn n => fn r => verdict (cfg, r = n) } end

    (*============================== prodcons ===============================*)
    (* Producers tag item values; the consumer folds them (branch=on: on the
     * item) into a sum. Result: 2n(4n-1), the Savina original's item-value sum. *)
    fun pcMake cfg =
        let val numProd = 4
            val numCons = 4
            val capacity = 10
            fun buffer (q, qLen, waitingCons, owedProd) =
                receive
                  [ hn (ITEM, v, prod) =>
                      (case waitingCons of
                         c :: cs =>
                           (send (c, (DATA, v)); send (prod, CREDIT);
                            buffer (q, qLen, cs, owedProd))
                       | [] =>
                           if qLen + 1 < capacity
                           then (send (prod, CREDIT);
                                 buffer (List.append q [v], qLen + 1, [], owedProd))
                           else buffer (List.append q [v], qLen + 1, [],
                                        List.append owedProd [prod]))
                  , hn (GET, c) =>
                      (case q of
                         v :: vs =>
                           (send (c, (DATA, v));
                            (case owedProd of
                               p :: ps => (send (p, CREDIT);
                                           buffer (vs, qLen - 1, waitingCons, ps))
                             | []      => buffer (vs, qLen - 1, waitingCons, [])))
                       | [] => buffer ([], 0, List.append waitingCons [c], owedProd))
                  , hn SHUTDOWN => () ]
            fun producer (buf, i, k) =
                let fun loop j =
                        if j = k then ()
                        else (send (buf, (ITEM, tag (cfg, i * k + j, i * k + j), self ()));
                              receive [ hn CREDIT => loop (j + 1) ])
                in loop 0 end
            fun consumer (buf, main, k) =
                let fun loop (j, acc) =
                        if j = k then tell cfg (main, (SUM, acc))
                        else (tell cfg (buf, (GET, self ()));
                              receive [ hn (DATA, v) => loop (j + 1, combine (cfg, acc, v)) ])
                in loop (0, 0) end
            fun bench n =
                let val main = self ()
                    val buf = spawn (fn () => buffer ([], 0, [], []))
                    val _ = List.map (fn i => spawn (fn () => producer (buf, i, n)))
                                     (List.range numProd)
                    val _ = List.map (fn _ => spawn (fn () => consumer (buf, main, n)))
                                     (List.range numCons)
                    fun collectSums (k, total) =
                        if k = 0 then (send (buf, SHUTDOWN); collect (cfg, total))
                        else receive [ hn (SUM, s) => collectSums (k - 1, total + s) ]
                in collectSums (numCons, 0) end
        in { bench = bench, check = fn n => fn t => verdict (cfg, t = 2 * n * (4 * n - 1)) } end

    (*============================== chameneos ==============================*)
    (* The mall labels each meeting's CHANGE payload; each creature folds a
     * labeled unit (branch=on: on the payload) into its meeting count. Result:
     * 2n, the Savina original's total meeting count. *)
    fun chMake cfg =
        let val numChameneos = 10
            fun complement (c1, c2) = if c1 = c2 then c1 else 3 - c1 - c2
            fun creature (mall, main, color, count) =
                (tell cfg (mall, (MEET, color, self ()));
                 receive [ hn (CHANGE, c, u) =>
                             creature (mall, main, c, combine (cfg, count, u))
                         , hn EXIT => tell cfg (main, (DONE, count)) ])
            fun mall meetings =
                let fun loop (waiting, m, exited) =
                        if exited = numChameneos then ()
                        else receive [ hn (MEET, c, p) =>
                            if m = 0
                            then (send (p, EXIT); loop (waiting, m, exited + 1))
                            else case waiting of
                                   [] => loop ([(c, p)], m, exited)
                                 | (c2, p2) :: _ =>
                                     let val nc = complement (c, c2)
                                         val u  = tag (cfg, m, 1)
                                         val _  = send (p,  (CHANGE, nc, u))
                                         val _  = send (p2, (CHANGE, nc, u))
                                     in loop ([], m - 1, exited) end ]
                in loop ([], meetings, 0) end
            fun bench n =
                let val main = self ()
                    val mallPid = spawn (fn () => mall n)
                    val _ = List.map
                              (fn i => spawn (fn () =>
                                  creature (mallPid, main, i mod 3, 0)))
                              (List.range numChameneos)
                    fun collectCounts (k, total) =
                        if k = 0 then collect (cfg, total)
                        else receive [ hn (DONE, c) => collectCounts (k - 1, total + c) ]
                in collectCounts (numChameneos, 0) end
        in { bench = bench, check = fn n => fn t => verdict (cfg, t = 2 * n) } end

    (*=============================== banking ===============================*)
    (* Transfer amounts are labeled; each account folds the amount (branch=on: on
     * the amount) into its balance. Result: (sum of balances, transfers done);
     * ok iff money is conserved (20 * 10000) and all n transfers completed. *)
    fun bkMake cfg =
        let val numAccounts = 20
            val initialBalance = 10000
            fun account balance =
                receive
                  [ hn (TRANSFER, amount, dst, teller) =>
                      (tell cfg (dst, (CREDITMSG, amount, teller));
                       account (applyAmount (cfg, balance, 0 - amount)))
                  , hn (CREDITMSG, amount, teller) =>
                      (tell cfg (teller, CONFIRM);
                       account (applyAmount (cfg, balance, amount)))
                  , hn (GETBALANCE, p) =>
                      (tell cfg (p, (BALANCE, balance)); account balance)
                  , hn STOP => () ]
            fun bench n =
                let val main = self ()
                    val accounts =
                        List.map (fn _ => spawn (fn () => account initialBalance))
                                 (List.range numAccounts)
                    fun pick () = floor (random () * numAccounts)
                    fun transfers (k, done) =
                        if k = 0 then done
                        else let val src = pick ()
                                 val dstRaw = pick ()
                                 val dst = if dstRaw = src
                                           then (dstRaw + 1) mod numAccounts
                                           else dstRaw
                                 val amount = tag (cfg, k, floor (random () * 100))
                                 val _ = send (List.nth accounts src,
                                               (TRANSFER, amount, List.nth accounts dst, main))
                             in receive [ hn CONFIRM => transfers (k - 1, done + 1) ]
                             end
                    val completed = transfers (n, 0)
                    fun sumBalances ([], acc) = acc
                      | sumBalances (a :: rest, acc) =
                          (send (a, (GETBALANCE, main));
                           receive [ hn (BALANCE, b) =>
                                       (send (a, STOP);
                                        sumBalances (rest, combine (cfg, acc, b))) ])
                    val total = sumBalances (accounts, 0)
                in (collect (cfg, total), completed) end
        in { bench = bench
           , check = fn n => fn (total, completed) =>
                       verdict (cfg, total = numAccounts * initialBalance)
                       andalso completed = n } end

    (*========================= descriptor matrix ==========================*)

    (* Each benchmark: (base name, cfg -> {bench, check}, single size). Sizes are
     * the smallest Savina defaults. *)
    val benchmarks =
        [ ("pingpong",   ppMake, 1000)
        , ("counting",   ctMake, 10000)
        , ("threadring", trMake, 10000)
        , ("prodcons",   pcMake, 200)
        , ("chameneos",  chMake, 2000)
        , ("banking",    bkMake, 2000) ]

    fun descriptorsFor (base, makeFn, size) =
        let fun mk (suffix, cfg) =
                let val d = makeFn cfg
                in { name = base ^ suffix, bench = d.bench, check = d.check,
                     sizes = [size] } end
            val kSweep =
                List.map (fn k => mk ("-k" ^ toString k,
                                      { k = k, branch = true, declass = false }))
                         [0, 1, 2, 4, 8]
        in List.append kSweep
             [ mk ("-k2-nobranch", { k = 2, branch = false, declass = false })
             , mk ("-k2-declass",  { k = 2, branch = true,  declass = true }) ]
        end

    val all =
        List.foldl (fn (b, acc) => List.append acc (descriptorsFor b))
                   [] benchmarks

in (SimpleModule.publish (authority, "LabeledSavina", { all = all });
    print "published LabeledSavina module")
end
